    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> on an island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a> in 1325 or 1327, and in 1521 Spanish forces and their Indigenous allies besieged and razed it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a> surrendered in August.</li><li>Cortés founded the Spanish capital on the ruins, and for three centuries Mexico City was the seat of the viceroyalty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, its archbishopric and its merchants, and afterwards the capital of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</li><li>The city sat on a lake bed with no natural outlet, so floods led to a drainage works begun in 1607 and finished under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a>, while later growth overflowed the Federal District and the soft clay began to sink.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The colonial city was built on the saturated clay of the former lake bed, and since the beginning of the 20th century it has sunk as much as nine metres in some areas and continues to sink almost 50 centimetres a year.</p></aside>
    </section>

