    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1325</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> on an island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a>, a date some sources give as 1327</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1521</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a> surrenders after the three-month siege and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> is razed, and Cortés refounds the city as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1607</span><span class="rf-text">Thousands of Indigenous workers are drafted to dig the first Desagüe, the drainage ditch and tunnel</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1821</span><span class="rf-text">The Army of the Three Guarantees enters the city and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> is declared independent</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1847</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> troops under Winfield Scott take the city after the battles of Molino del Rey and Chapultepec</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1913</span><span class="rf-text">The Decena Trágica ends with the coup of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoriano-huerta">Victoriano Huerta</a> and the killing of Francisco Madero</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1985</span><span class="rf-text">An earthquake of magnitude 8.1 collapses hundreds of buildings</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1997</span><span class="rf-text">The city elects its mayor for the first time since 1928</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/carlos-de-siguenza-y-gongora"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlos-de-siguenza-y-gongora.jpg" alt="Carlos de Sigüenza y Góngora" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/carlos-de-siguenza-y-gongora">Carlos de Sigüenza y Góngora</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Don_Carlos_de_Sig%C3%BCenza_y_G%C3%B3ngora.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">D. Alfredo Chavero, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city stands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>, a basin in the Trans-Mexican Volcanic Belt on the high plateaus of south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. The valley has no natural drainage outlet, and for most of its history the site lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a>, a system of interconnected salt and freshwater lakes. The oldest signs of human occupation in the area are those of the Peñón woman, whom a 2003 study dated to 12,700 years ago. After the fall of the Toltec capital of Tollan, large migrations of Nahuatl-speaking peoples entered the valley and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> around the lake. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> were the last wave, and the peoples already there resisted their coming.</p><p>The Mexica founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> on an island on the western side of the lake in 1325 or 1327. Their own story is that the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huitzilopochtli">Huitzilopochtli</a> led them there after a long migration and marked the place with an eagle perched on a prickly pear cactus, to which later versions add a snake in its beak. Three codices place the founding in 1325, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-de-siguenza-y-gongora">Carlos de Sigüenza y Góngora</a> gives the day 18 July 1327. A group left the island after internal strife and set up a monarchy of its own at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a>, which Tenochtitlan later conquered and absorbed. From 1430 the Mexica and their allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a> conquered the Valley of Mexico and took tribute from the peoples who had resisted them. Tenochtitlan was laid out with streets and canals on the cardinal directions, in four districts, and it roughly corresponds to the historic centre of the present city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/axayacatl.jpg" alt="Axayacatl" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl">Axayacatl</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Axayacatl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> is estimated to have held at least 100,000 and perhaps as many as 200,000 people when the Spaniards first saw it in 1519. The Spanish city that replaced it grew as the seat of power. Cortés took over the old palaces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl">Axayacatl</a> and Moctezuma for his own houses on the main square, later called the Plaza Mayor or Zócalo,. The Franciscan friar Toribio de Benavente Motolinia counted the rebuilding of the city among the afflictions of the early years, with many workers killed by falling beams or in the tearing down of old buildings. By the 1530s Mexico City had jurisdiction over other town councils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> was founded in 1553 and the merchants' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>, the consulado, in 1594, and from the late 1560s until 1813 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila galleon</a> carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> in exchange for Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>.</p><p>In the later 18th century nobles who had made fortunes in mining or on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> elsewhere spent them in the capital on mansions, which gave the city its nickname of the City of Palaces. An estimate for 1689 was 57,000, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of Viceroy Revillagigedo in 1790 counted 112,926 residents. In the 19th century the city spread by extending its rectangular grid and in 1865 Maximilian had a wide avenue laid out, later the Paseo de la Reforma. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a>, from 1876, the city gained better roads, schools and transport. The city had about 500,000 people in 1900 and then spread west and south, and between 1960 and 1980 its population more than doubled to 8,831,079, overflowing the boundaries of the Federal District.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Charles_V,_Holy_Roman_Emperor,_seated_(1500%E2%80%931558),_formerly_attributed_to_Titian_(Alte_Pinakothek,_Munich).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> was ruled by its tlatoani, Moctezuma being its ruler when the Spaniards arrived. After the siege of 1521 Cortés first settled at Coyoacán and made the new Spanish capital on the island, and although he did not set up a territory under his own rule and stayed loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, the emperor did not appoint him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. The viceroyalty of New Spain was seated in the city, as were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> and the archbishopric. Two Indigenous sections, San Juan Tenochtitlan and Santiago <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a>, were governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> governors and councils, and the city was designated a city of Spaniards.</p><p>On 21 September 1821 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a>'s Army of the Three Guarantees entered the city, and independence was declared on 27 September. Iturbide was crowned emperor in the cathedral on 21 July 1822 and abdicated in the spring of 1823, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> became a republic. The constitution of 1824 made Mexico City the capital, and Congress set apart a federal district around the Zócalo on 18 November 1824. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> forces occupied the city after 1847. In the Reform War of 1857 to 1861 the Liberals governed from Veracruz and the Conservatives from the capital, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> moved his government there. Maximilian ruled the Second Mexican Empire from 1864 to 1867, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a> governed Mexico from 1876 to 1910. The city lost its elected mayor and council in 1928, when the president began to appoint the mayor, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a> Cárdenas was elected in 1997. On 29 January 2016 the Federal District was renamed Ciudad de México.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-de-sahagun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernardino-de-sahagun.jpg" alt="Bernardino de Sahagún" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-de-sahagun">Bernardino de Sahagún</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernardino_de_Sahag%C3%BAn_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the conquest Spaniards lived in the traza around the Zócalo, in orderly streets, and Indigenous people lived in sections outside it, though Indigenous traders used the Zócalo and the Spanish effort to keep the areas apart was hard to enforce. The 1793 count of the city, which omits officials, listed 50,371 Spaniards (48.1 per cent), 25,603 Indians (24.4 per cent), 19,357 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizos</a>, 7,094 mulattos and 2,335 Europeans. It had the largest concentration of people of Spanish descent and of mixed ancestry in the colony. The city also held many poor people. The Nacional Monte de Piedad, a pawnshop giving interest-free small loans, was set up in 1777 by the Count of Regla, and the Royal Cigar Factory gave thousands of jobs to the urban poor, women among them. Revillagigedo cleared pulque stalls and animals from the Plaza Mayor, paved and lit it, and had the entrance of the Alameda guarded so that poorly clad people could not enter.</p><p>The city was the centre of religious power. The cult of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-guadalupe">Virgin of Guadalupe</a> is said to have originated in a vision at Tepeyac Hill in 1531, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-de-sahagun">Bernardino de Sahagún</a> suspected it of continuing the Aztec cult of Tonantzin. The Colegio de Santa Cruz de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a> is recognised as the first European school of higher learning in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a> muralism began in the city, and works by Orozco, Siqueiros and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-rivera">Diego Rivera</a> stand in the National Palace and the Palacio de Bellas Artes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomi</a>, Mixtec, Zapotec and Mazahua are the Indigenous languages with most speakers there today.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado.jpg" alt="Pedro de Alvarado" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_de_Alvarado_(Tom%C3%A1s_Povedano).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tomás Povedano, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The conquest was a siege. Cortés entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> on 8 November 1519, was received by Moctezuma and held him under house arrest. While Cortés was away, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> ordered a massacre during the spring festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huitzilopochtli">Huitzilopochtli</a>. Moctezuma was wounded by stones and arrows from his own people and died soon after; whether the Spanish killed him is not known. On the night of 30 June 1520, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-noche-triste">La Noche Triste</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> drove the Spaniards and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans">Tlaxcalans</a> out, killing at least 600 Spaniards. Cortés besieged the city from May 1521. For three months it suffered from lack of food and water and from disease brought by the Europeans, and Spanish forces and their allies fought through it street by street until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a> surrendered in August 1521. The dikes were destroyed and Tenochtitlan was razed.</p><p>The lake caused floods, and from 1607 thousands of Indigenous people were drafted to dig the Desagüe. In 1642 the Inquisition arrested 150 crypto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and on 11 April 1649 twelve were burned after being strangled and one alive. Riots in 1624 and 1692 ousted a viceroy and burned the residences of the archbishop and the viceroy. In September 1847 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> troops took the city. During the Decena Trágica of February 1913 the centre was shelled and Madero was shot after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huerta">Huerta</a>'s coup. Government troops killed an unknown number of protesting students at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a> in 1968, and the 1985 earthquake collapsed 412 buildings and killed about 10,000 people, a figure in dispute.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zócalo, the old Aztec ceremonial centre, became the Plaza Mayor of the colony and is now the central square of the nation, with the Metropolitan Cathedral on one side. Many colonial buildings and palaces remain in the historic centre and serve as government buildings and museums. The Ciudad Universitaria of the National Autonomous University, built from 1950 to 1953 with murals by Rivera and Siqueiros, was named a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2007, and the Metro opened its first line in 1969. The soft clay of the former lake bed is collapsing as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> is over-extracted, and the city has sunk as much as nine metres in some areas since the start of the 20th century. From the 1940s a freeze on rents, repealed in 1998, left the centre's buildings to decay, and the area lost about 100,000 residents before efforts to restore it began in the early 2000s.</p><p>Mexico City is today the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and about 9.2 million people live in the city proper and about 21.8 million in Greater Mexico City, which extends into the State of Mexico and Hidalgo. Its centre sits on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, whose name endured in the Indigenous section of San Juan Tenochtitlan and in the eagle and cactus at the centre of the Mexican flag.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mexico City is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> there in 1325 or 1327, and after Spanish forces and their allies razed it in 1521 Cortés built the colonial capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> on its ruins. It became the capital of independent Mexico in 1821.</em></p>

